 analysing brain networks contribute to reproducible and open neuroimaging workflows collaborate with researchers within the Connectome Lab, CNCR and international research networks present your findings at national and international scientific meetings write scientific manuscripts contribute to teaching activities where appropriate Your profile MSc degree in neuroscience, neuroimaging, genetics, biomedical engineering, physics, computer science, artificial intelligence, mathematics, psychology, biology, or a related quantitative discipline. Hands-on experience with computational analysis is important. Ideally, you have experience with several of the following: structural MRI and cortical reconstruction, including FreeSurfer Genetics (GWAS) diffusion-weighted MRI (DWI), tractography and structural connectivity resting-state fMRI and functional connectivity analysis FSL and other commonly used neuroimaging software packages Experience with AI / LLM processing and quality control of neuroimaging datasets connectome and network neuroscience analyses Strong interest in and experience with scientific programming is essential. You should have experience with Python, MATLAB, R, Bash and/or comparable languages , and be comfortable working with computational analysis pipelines. Experience with one or more of the following would be an advantage: large-scale neuroimaging datasets such as UK Biobank, HCP or comparable resources comparative or cross-species neuroimaging brain atlases, surface-based analysis and registration machine learning or multivariate statistics high-performance computing version control and reproducible research workflows network science and computational modelling We are particularly interested in candidates who enjoy working with complex data, think critically about methodology, and are motivated to develop strong computational and neuroimaging expertise. You should also: have a strong interest in neurodiversity and/or evolution be able to work independently while enjoying collaboration within a multidisciplinary team have good analytical and problem-solving skills have a good command of written and spoken English be motivated to learn new neuroimaging and computational techniques support open and reproducible science and be willing to share tools and data with the wider scientific community As a university, we strive for equal opportunities for all, recognising that diversity takes many forms. We believe that diversity in all its complexity is invaluable for the quality of our teaching, research and service. We are always looking for talent with diverse backgrounds and experiences. This also means that we are committed to creating an inclusive community so that we can use diversity as an asset. We realise that each individual brings a unique set of skills, expertise and mindset. Therefore we are happy to invite anyone who recognises themselves in the profile to apply, even if you do not meet all the requirements. What do we offer? A challenging position in a socially engaged organisation. At VU Amsterdam, you contribute to education, research and service for a better world. And that is valuable. So in return for your efforts, we offer you: a salary of minimum € 3.204,00 (PhD year 1) and maximum € 4.051 (PhD year 4) gross per month, on a full-time basis. This is based on UFO profile PhD candidate. a 4-year PhD position (initial 1-year contract, extended by 3 years following positive evaluation). We also offer you attractive fringe benefits and regulations. Some examples: a full-time 38-hour working week comes with a holiday leave entitlement of 232 hours per year. If you choose to work 40 hours, you have 96 extra holiday leave hours on an annual basis. For part-timers, this is calculated pro rata. 8% holiday allowance and 8.3% end-of-year bonus solid pension scheme (ABP) contribution to commuting expenses optional model for designing a personalized benefits package About us Research environment You will join the C